<mn>13</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>24</mn></math>. Adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction s Over 13 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>13</mn></mfrac></math> to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"7 equals negative 24 plus StartFraction s Over 13 EndFraction\"><mn>7</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>13</mn></mfrac></math>. Adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"24\"><mn>24</mn></math> to both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"31 equals StartFraction s Over 13 EndFraction\"><mn>31</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>13</mn></mfrac></math>. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"403 equals s\"><mn>403</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></math>. Therefore, if the equation has infinitely many solutions, the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"s\"><mi>s</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"403\"><mn>403</mn></math>.</p>","externalid":"ad6cdfd4-4643-45b2-b72d-0602fb30ce1c","correct_answer":["403"]},"createDate":1755117017722,"updateDate":1755117017722}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017722, 1755117017722),
    ($SATQ$adb0c96c$SATQ$, $SATQ$e8d1c0cc-b06d-4425-92fb-3beba3af4800$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$028fa3b1-8639-4513-b83d-68cfcccae859$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Systems of two linear equations in two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: center;"><math alttext="24 x plus y equals 48"><mrow>
	<mrow>
